Three cases make the physics clear.

Current with the wind or against it. A current running straight downwind carries both tacks the same amount the wrong way, and also takes away from the wind over the water (2 knots of current in 12 knots of wind leaves 10). Running against the wind it does the opposite: the boat is carried upwind and also gets more wind to sail in. Both tacks are affected equally.
Current across the wind. Now the current carries both tacks sideways by the same amount: on its own it favours neither. But the wind over the water has swung, towards the side the current comes from. With 2 knots of current across 12 knots of wind it swings by about 9°. For the tack heading into the current that is a lift, a wind shift that lets it point closer to the true upwind direction; for the other tack it is a header. In that case the favoured tack makes 5.7 knots towards the wind over the ground and the other 4.4.
Lee-bowing. The favoured tack is the one with the current coming against its lee bow, the bow on the side away from the wind. Sailors have long known that “lee-bowing the tide” pays, and the usual explanation is that the current pushes the boat to windward. The calculation says otherwise: the current’s push is the same on either tack. What differs is the wind the boat sails in, and the lee-bowed tack gets the lift. (A current that varies across the course, stronger in one place than another, adds real differences between tacks, which this uniform current does not.)

Current also changes the waves. Where the current runs against the wind, the waves it raises meet a current that slows them and squeezes them shorter, and they grow steeper: the short, breaking sea of wind against tide that every coastal sailor knows. Where wind and current run together the sea flattens out. The 3D views in these posts use waves for still water.

Wind, sea and the Beaufort scale
Sailors describe the wind by its Beaufort force, a scale drawn up by Francis Beaufort, a Royal Navy officer, in 1805 for describing the effect of the wind on a warship’s sails, and later tied to measured wind speeds and to the state of the sea. The J/109’s certificates cover forces 2 to 6:

bftTable = {
  const cur = bft(tws)[0];
  return htl.html`&lt;table class="sl-table"&gt;&lt;thead&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;th&gt;Force&lt;/th&gt;&lt;th&gt;Description&lt;/th&gt;&lt;th&gt;Wind (knots)&lt;/th&gt;&lt;th&gt;Sea&lt;/th&gt;&lt;th&gt;Open-sea waves, probable (max), m&lt;/th&gt;&lt;th&gt;Waves in 20 km of fetch, m&lt;/th&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;/thead&gt;&lt;tbody&gt;
    ${BEAUFORT.slice(1).map(b =&gt; htl.html`&lt;tr style=${b[0] === cur ? "background:rgba(231,111,81,.15);font-weight:600" : ""}&gt;
      &lt;td class="num"&gt;${b[0]}&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt;${b[1]}&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td class="num"&gt;${b[3]}–${b[4]}&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt;${b[5]}&lt;/td&gt;
      &lt;td class="num"&gt;${fmt(b[6], 1)} (${fmt(b[7], 1)})&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td class="num"&gt;${fmt(seaState(b[2]).hs, seaState(b[2]).hs &lt; 0.1 ? 2 : 1)}&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;`)}
  &lt;/tbody&gt;&lt;/table&gt;`;
}






The Met Office’s wave heights are for a sea that has had the wind blowing over it long enough, and over enough open water, to be fully developed. Most racing happens in coastal waters where the fetch, the distance of open water upwind, is much shorter, and the waves stay smaller and shorter. The standard relations for a fetch-limited wind sea, from the JONSWAP North Sea wave project, give the significant wave height \(H_s\) and the peak period \(T_p\) from the wind speed \(U\) and the fetch \(F\):
\[
\frac{g H_s}{U^2} = 1.6\times10^{-3}\left(\frac{gF}{U^2}\right)^{1/2}, \qquad \frac{g T_p}{U} = 0.286\left(\frac{gF}{U^2}\right)^{1/3},
\]
and the wavelength of deep-water waves of period \(T\) is \(gT^2/2\pi\). The 3D view uses these for 20 km of fetch, with a few wave trains travelling downwind at their own speeds and white crests from force 4, and the boat pitching, rolling and heaving on them. The waves are for show: the speed model assumes flat water. Waves slow a real boat, most of all upwind, where it climbs every one of them; that is one reason the ORC’s own program is more detailed than this one.

